"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][37]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M37-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M37-L1 – Research Question, Literature and a Testable Hypothesis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Optional Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32-L6<\/a>\nor another completed Master’s specialisation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Develop a focused research\nquestion with a falsifiable hypothesis, a comparison and a documented\nreview of the literature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the engineering problem to the question<\/h2>\n<p>A research project begins not with the choice of a programming\nlanguage but with a question whose answer is not yet known. A\ndevelopment project may aim to produce a working solution; a scientific\ninvestigation must additionally show under what conditions that solution\nworks, how it differs from alternatives and what uncertainties remain.\nBoth objectives can coincide in one project.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a domain familiar from your studies so far. For example, “I\nwant to build a better temperature controller” becomes “How accurately\ndoes a first-order thermal model predict the temperature after a change\nin heater power if it was identified using only a single power level?”\nThe question limits the investigation to one model, one input, one\noutput and a test range. It can be investigated even without laboratory\nhardware. The following module uses synthetic data and tests methods,\nnot an actual plant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_108_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Five steps from the research question through the hypothesis to a predefined test plan.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A testable research project links its question,\nexisting knowledge, hypothesis and predefined acceptance checks; the\nthermal example sets measurable error limits.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State of research and literature review<\/h2>\n<p>Start with two or three precise search terms, such as\n<code>first order thermal system identification step response<\/code> and\n<code>least squares residual validation<\/code>. Look for textbooks on\nfundamentals, review articles for orientation and original papers on\nspecific methods. Document the search date, search terms, database and\ninclusion and exclusion criteria. Follow citations forwards and\nbackwards; check whether a claim actually applies to the stated boundary\nconditions. An abstract is insufficient for the details of an\nexperiment. A university curriculum establishes the educational\norientation and a methodology handbook supports the calculation\nprocedure; neither replaces your own experiment.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evidence needed<\/th>\n<th>Suitable source<\/th>\n<th>Your own check<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What deliverables belong to a university project?<\/td>\n<td>ETH project description<\/td>\n<td>Plan for a report and presentation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How are model parameters estimated?<\/td>\n<td>Methodological literature on least squares<\/td>\n<td>Check the error function and identifiability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What makes a calculation traceable?<\/td>\n<td>The Turing Way<\/td>\n<td>Record code, data, versions and execution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Your literature matrix should include author, year, source location,\nclaim, assumptions, method and relevance. Paraphrases require references\njust as quotations do. When findings conflict, compare the application\ndomain and method instead of selecting only the claim you want.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hypothesis and counter-test<\/h2>\n<p>Our example compares the dynamic model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=KP(1-e^{-t/\\tau})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a static baseline\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=K_sP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the temperature rise in K,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe constant heater power in W,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe steady-state thermal gain in K/W and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na time constant in s. Both models are to be estimated from the same 3 W\nexperiment.<\/p>\n<p>The engineering hypothesis: in the unchanged system, the dynamic\nmodel achieves an RMSE of at most 0.30 K and at most half the RMSE of\nthe static baseline on an independent 5 W test run. As a counter-test,\nan additional test increases the time constant; the model is allowed to\nfail here. These thresholds are chosen for teaching purposes, not\nprescribed by a standard, and do not constitute a statistical\nsignificance test. The time series includes time zero, so the baseline\nwithout dynamics is a deliberately simple comparison.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Choosing your own topic<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Specialisation<\/th>\n<th>Question as a starting point<\/th>\n<th>Quantity to check<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M32<\/a><\/td>\n<td>Does MPC still offer advantages under changed parameters?<\/td>\n<td>Control error and actuator limits<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M33<\/a><\/td>\n<td>How sensitive is power flow to local injections?<\/td>\n<td>Power balance, voltage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34<\/a><\/td>\n<td>When does jitter rather than quantisation dominate?<\/td>\n<td>SINAD, ENOB<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M35<\/a><\/td>\n<td>At what data rate does an accelerator become worthwhile?<\/td>\n<td>Latency, deadline misses<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M36&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M36<\/a><\/td>\n<td>How do different failure assumptions change expected supply\ninterruptions?<\/td>\n<td>Expected energy not supplied, uncertainty<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Define what lies outside the project. Simulated execution times do\nnot justify direct claims about real hardware; modelled failure\nstatistics do not guarantee real-grid performance. Fix the question,\nprimary metric, data split and acceptance rule before the main analysis.\nLater exploratory changes are permitted but must be identifiable as\nsuch.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Formulate a question for your specialisation with an input, an\noutput and a comparison. <strong>Check:<\/strong> The answer could also\nbe negative; the test range is stated.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens if the model achieves only 0.31 K RMSE but performs\ntwice as well as the baseline? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The combined\nacceptance rule is not satisfied.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a simulated test with changed parameters a physical measurement?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No; it tests the response within the chosen\nmodel.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ee.ethz.ch/studies/master-s-programmes/main-master/projects-and-master-thesis.html\">ETH\nZurich – Projects and Master’s Thesis (Curriculum G8)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Semester\nprojects section: supervision, written report and presentation; not\nevidence for a specific research method. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/reproducible-research/\">The\nTuring Way – Guide for Reproducible Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Guide for\nReproducible Research, introduction: reproducible analysis using\navailable data and code; methodological aspect. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/pmd.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Engineering Statistics Handbook, Process Modeling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 4,\nintroduction and purpose of modeling; methodological context. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M37-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M37-L2 – Experiment or Simulation Plan with Metrics and Reference Methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Optional Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M37-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Specify experimental conditions,\nindependent test data, metrics and verification calculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental design<\/h2>\n<p>A good plan makes clear in advance which inputs are actively varied,\nwhich influences are kept constant and which outputs are evaluated. For\na measurement, this includes a wiring diagram, calibrated instruments,\nsafe operating limits and environmental conditions. In a simulation, the\ncorresponding elements are model equations, parameters and initial\nconditions. A reference method should not share the same possible\nimplementation fault as the method under investigation.<\/p>\n<p>For our thermal system,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a constant power step at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napply. The model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot y+y=KP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the normalised heat balance with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=R_{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=R_{th}C_{th}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe synthetic observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i=y(t_i)+\\epsilon_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses independent normally distributed noise with standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK. A real measurement series may also contain offset, drift, thermal lag\nof the sensor and correlated noise; none of these is claimed or modelled\nhere.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">P (W)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">True K (K/W)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">True τ (s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Seed<\/th>\n<th>Purpose<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fitting<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3101<\/td>\n<td>Determine parameters<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal test<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3102<\/td>\n<td>Check acceptance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deviation<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3103<\/td>\n<td>Check limits of validity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>There are 31 values per experiment, at times from 0 to 60 s in steps\nof 2 s. All values are synthetic. The model parameters are estimated\nonly from the fitting dataset; the nominal test and deviation datasets\nremain separate. The final experiment is not used to improve the model\nretrospectively.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_109_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three separate experiments: fitting, nominal testing and testing under model deviation.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: A fitting dataset and two independent tests separate\nparameter identification, nominal validation and deliberate model\nmismatch; the three experiments serve distinct evidential\npurposes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison metrics<\/h2>\n<p>For observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand prediction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i=z_i-\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called the residual. The mean error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar e=\\sum_i e_i/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicates a directional bias. The RMSE is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><msubsup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{RMSE}=\\sqrt{\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n e_i^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It has the same unit as the measured quantity (K), weights large\ndeviations disproportionately and, on its own, can conceal important\npatterns in a temporally correlated series. For errors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1,0,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, the result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar e=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, but an RMSE of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.816<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2/3}=0.816<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK. Supplement it as needed with maximum error, mean absolute error and\nresiduals over time; do not use percentage errors for temperature rises\nnear zero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference calculation and checks<\/h2>\n<p>The analytical solution for a constant input is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=KP(1-e^{-t/\\tau})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA second computational method is the exact discrete recursion when the\ninput is constant within a time interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=e^{-h/\\tau},\\qquad y_{i+1}=ay_i+(1-a)KP.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Compare both methods with the same parameters. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.84648<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\approx0.84648<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first step at 3 W and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK/W is approximately 0.9211 K. This agreement checks the implementation,\nnot the validity of the physical model. Additionally, for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nevery noise-free value must be zero; at long times, the response\napproaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">KP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data splitting and sources of error<\/h2>\n<p>Randomly splitting adjacent time points can lead to overly optimistic\nresults with real, correlated data. Hold back entire experimental runs\nor independent operating conditions. If you change the model structure,\nparameter range or acceptance threshold based on the test data, that\ntest becomes a development dataset; obtain a new independent test.\nRepeating the run with the same seed checks reproducibility; repeating\nit with a different seed checks sensitivity to random influences. In the\nlaboratory, sequence, heating, drift and repeatability must also be\nplanned for. Safety-critical, mains-voltage or high-energy experiments\nrequire qualified laboratory supervision; this self-study project stays\nwithin simulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the 5 W test profile not be used for parameter\noptimisation? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The prediction test would lose\nits independence.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the steady-state value at 5 W. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\nA temperature rise of 10 K.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Design another counter-test. <strong>Check:<\/strong> For example, a\nsequence of several power steps using exact recursion; define the\nscenario and metric beforehand.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/pmd.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Engineering Statistics Handbook, Process Modeling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter index\n4.3: experimental design and assessment; subsections not fully reviewed.\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section4/pmd431.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Least Squares<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.4.3.1: parameter estimation by minimizing\nsquared residuals; partial scope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/reproducible-research/\">The\nTuring Way – Guide for Reproducible Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Guide for\nReproducible Research, introduction: reproducible analysis using\navailable data and code; methodological aspect. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M37-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M37-L3 – Reproducible Implementation: Data Provenance, Experimental Log, Configuration and Analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Optional Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M37-L2<\/a>,\nPython and elementary linear algebra.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reproduce the data and\nparameters of a model comparison and carry out an independent numerical\ncheck.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A computational archive rather than a single results\nfigure<\/h2>\n<p>Another researcher should be able to identify the input data,\nsoftware versions and settings that produced a result. Keep raw data\nunchanged, separate processing from the raw data and generate tables and\ndiagrams from the same dataset. For synthetic data, the generator is\nalso part of the method. For measured data, include units, sensor\nidentity, sampling time, calibration, missing values and how they are\nhandled. Checksums can detect unintended changes, but do not prove that\ndata were collected correctly.<\/p>\n<p>A minimal project may contain the files <code>README.md<\/code>,\n<code>config.json<\/code>, <code>data/raw/<\/code>, <code>run.py<\/code>,\n<code>results/<\/code>, <code>report.md<\/code> and a list of versions.\nThis teaching experiment is deliberately implemented as a single\nexecutable code block; its configuration appears at the beginning and in\n<code>main()<\/code>. For your own project, also export the data and\nresults and document the provenance of the generated files.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_110_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Data flow from model parameters and random seed through estimation to results and checksum.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Configuration, data, parameter estimation and\nvalidation form a reproducible processing chain; fixed random seeds and\nchecksums help trace the particular computational run.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Parameter estimation<\/h2>\n<p>The unknown parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K,\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare determined by minimising the squared errors on the fitting dataset.\nFor each fixed candidate time constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_i=P(1-e^{-t_i/\\tau})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis known and the problem is linear in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>K<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msubsup><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(K|\\tau)=\\sum_i(z_i-Kg_i)^2,\\qquad \\hat K(\\tau)=\\frac{\\sum_i g_iz_i}{\\sum_i g_i^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formula follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial Q/\\partial K=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i g_i^2&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe place\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a grid between 4 and 30 s in steps of 0.1 s and select the smallest\nerror. This is a controlled search space of known resolution; if the\noptimum lies at the boundary or there are competing minima, the estimate\nmust be investigated further. The static comparison minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i(z_i-K_sP)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>K<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>/<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat K_s=\\bar z/P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth methods receive the same data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complete computational experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Save the following block as <code>run.py<\/code> and execute\n<code>python run.py<\/code>. The original editorial team tested it with\nPython 3.13.5 and NumPy 2.3.5. The seeds are fixed and quantities are\nexpressed in units close to SI conventions. Numerical rounding\ndifferences may arise when libraries or platforms change.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> hashlib <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> sha256<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>T <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">62.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>TAUS <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">301<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>SIGMA <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.15<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> response(p, k, tau):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.expm1(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>T<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>tau))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> data(p, tau, seed):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> response(p, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, tau) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.random.default_rng(seed).normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, SIGMA, T.size)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> fit(p, y):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    basis <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.expm1(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>T[<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>,:]<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>TAUS[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    ks <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (basis <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> y)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(basis<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>basis, axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    sse <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>((basis<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>ks[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    i <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.argmin(sse)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> ks[i], TAUS[i]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> rmse(y, pred):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.sqrt(np.mean((y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>pred)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> main():<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    train <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> data(<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3101<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    k, tau <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> fit(<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, train)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    static <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> train.mean()<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;fit&#39;<\/span>, k, tau, static)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"co\"># Independent exact discrete state recursion, no closed-form response used here.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>tau)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> T[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        x.append(a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(np.array(x)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>response(<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>,k,tau))) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rows<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name,p,true_tau,seed <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;fit&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3101<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;test&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3102<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;shift&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">18.<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3103<\/span>)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> data(p,true_tau,seed)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        pred <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> response(p,k,tau)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        stat <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.full(T.size,p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>static)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        rows.append((name,rmse(y,pred),rmse(y,stat),np.mean(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>pred)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(name,<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>[<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>v<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    samples <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([fit(<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,data(<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>,s)) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3200<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3400<\/span>)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;quantiles&#39;<\/span>,np.quantile(samples,[<span class=\"fl\">.025<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.975<\/span>],axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;train_sha256&#39;<\/span>, sha256(train.astype(<span class=\"st\">&#39;&lt;f8&#39;<\/span>).tobytes()).hexdigest())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> rows[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> rows[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rows[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> rows[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> k,tau,static,rows,samples<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-48\"><a href=\"#cb1-48\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span> <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-49\"><a href=\"#cb1-49\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    main()<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The checksum refers to the 31 fitting values in little-endian Float64\nformat, not to a CSV file. The code separates the generation of each\ntest case from parameter estimation. The return value of\n<code>main()<\/code> enables further analysis without copying rounded\nresults; <code>rows<\/code> contains the three cases and\n<code>samples<\/code> the repeated estimates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference execution log<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quantity<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Calculated value<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dynamic gain K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.999979 K/W<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time constant τ<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11.9 s<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Static gain Ks<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.585786 K/W<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fitting RMSE, dynamic<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.128308 K<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal test RMSE, dynamic<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.167320 K<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal test RMSE, static<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.634260 K<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>According to the assertion, the analytical prediction and the exact\nstate recursion agree to within less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-12}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK. The data checksum is\n<code>6e7dca46941ea6879197265aeda97df4707cf47176da602efdb9f30313c44681<\/code>.\nThe static gain is smaller than the actual steady-state gain because it\napproximates the entire transient with a single constant value. The\ndynamic fit matches the synthetic model well; it does not independently\nestablish whether a real plant would be second-order.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Halve the grid spacing: what changes? <strong>Check:<\/strong> The\nestimate may become finer, but the information in the data does not\nincrease. Document a new run.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a checksum alone insufficient? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> It\nestablishes the identity of the byte sequence, not its technical\ncorrectness.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Imagine removing all times after 2 s. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> For\nearly times, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\approx KP t/\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nK and τ become difficult to identify separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section4/pmd431.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Least Squares<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.4.3.1: parameter estimation by minimizing\nsquared residuals; partial scope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/reproducible-research/\">The\nTuring Way – Guide for Reproducible Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Guide for\nReproducible Research, introduction: reproducible analysis using\navailable data and code; methodological aspect. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/compendia/\">The\nTuring Way – Research Compendia<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Research Compendia, Summary and\nMotivation: combining data, code and research documentation. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M37-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M37-L4 – Validation, Uncertainty and Critical Discussion of Results<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Optional Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M37-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish test error, random\neffects and structural model deviation, and establish the limits of\nvalidity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is actually demonstrated?<\/h2>\n<p>Verification examines whether the chosen equations and computational\nsteps have been implemented correctly. Validation asks whether the\nassumptions are adequate for the intended application. An independent\nanalytical calculation can detect numerical errors but cannot compensate\nfor missing physics. With synthetic test data, validation here means\nonly testing against the known simulated data-generating process; a real\nplant would require an independent experiment.<\/p>\n<p>The model from L3 meets both criteria from L1 in the nominal test:\n0.1673 K is below 0.30 K and less than half the static error of 2.6343\nK. With a changed time constant, the two criteria are not both met. It\nis precisely this check under changed conditions that reveals the\nmodel’s limits.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dataset<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Dynamic RMSE (K)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Static RMSE (K)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Mean dynamic residual (K)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fitting<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.128308<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.587489<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.001674<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal test<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.167320<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.634260<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.015644<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deviation<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.041416<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.829938<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">−0.927214<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The deviation case has a negative mean error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z-\\hat y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause the faster model overestimates the heating. With a real\nmeasurement, the cause would not automatically be known; it could, for\nexample, be a change in thermal capacitance or additional dynamics.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_111_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Error comparison for three cases and empirical variation of the time constant over 200 repetitions.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Three test cases compare model error with a\npredefined threshold; spread across 200 repetitions additionally\nillustrates uncertainty in the estimated time constant.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variability of parameter estimation<\/h2>\n<p>The same estimation procedure was repeated for 200 new synthetic\nfitting runs (seeds 3200 to 3399). K and τ in the data-generating\nprocess remained unchanged. The empirical quantiles from L3 are:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">2.5%<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Median<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">97.5%<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>K (K/W)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.96510<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.00052<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.03390<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>τ (s)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11.3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12.8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The quantiles describe the distribution of the estimates in this\nknown simulation. They are neither a confidence interval derived from a\nsingle real measurement nor a guarantee of coverage for other\ndisturbance models. If the data-generating process is unknown, a\nsuitable uncertainty method with justified assumptions must be used,\nsuch as a parametric model or, for dependent observations, an adapted\nresampling method. The repetitions examine only random variability, not\nsystematic bias.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensitivity and model limits<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=KP(1-e^{-t/\\tau})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>τ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial K}=P(1-e^{-t/\\tau}),\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\tau}=-\\frac{KPt}{\\tau^2}e^{-t/\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At time zero, both derivatives are zero. Late in the experiment,\nsensitivity to τ is small, whereas sensitivity to K approaches P. For\nfixed τ, the magnitude of the derivative with respect to τ is greatest\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\tau=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns, the signed derivative with respect to τ is approximately −0.3066 K/s.\nThe time constant therefore affects the transient in particular. A\ndataset covering both the early transient and the later approach to the\nfinal value allows substantially better joint estimation than\nsteady-state data alone.<\/p>\n<p>For small parameter uncertainties, the parameter covariance matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma_\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the prediction gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be used to estimate the linearised variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\Sigma_\\theta J^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a new measured value, the variance of independent additive noise\nmust also be included. With large deviations and an incorrect model\nstructure, linear uncertainty propagation is no substitute for new\ntests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Discussion and decision<\/h2>\n<p>Report negative as well as positive results. Do not write “model\nvalidated” without specifying its domain of validity. A suitable\nstatement is: the dynamic approach meets the predefined criteria for the\nnominal synthetic 5 W case, but not at τ=18 s. For applications outside\nthis range, further data and possibly renewed identification or an\nextended model structure are needed. Check residuals for trends; a small\nmean deviation does not rule out periodic errors. In real experiments,\nalso examine instrument resolution, offset, repeatability, temperature\nrange and dependence between observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the relative RMSE reduction in the nominal test?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.167320<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2.634260<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>93.65<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.167320/2.634260\\approx93.65\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is failure in the deviation case valuable?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> It limits the scope of application; small\nstatistical variability in the nominal model tells us nothing about this\nlimit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What information is missing for the uncertainty of a real\ntemperature value? <strong>Check:<\/strong> Calibration, instrument\nspecifications, temporal correlation, environmental influences and model\ndeviations are among the factors.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/pmd.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Engineering Statistics Handbook, Process Modeling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter index\n4.4: model selection, fitting and validation; subsections not fully\nreviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmd/section1/pmd132.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\n– Prediction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.1.3.2: prediction and distinction of prediction\nuncertainty. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/reproducible-research/\">The\nTuring Way – Guide for Reproducible Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Guide for\nReproducible Research, introduction: reproducible analysis using\navailable data and code; methodological aspect. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M37-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M37-L5 – Technical Report, Presentation and Technical Self-assessment<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M37 – Optional Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Research and Final Project<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M37-L1<\/a>\nthrough <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M37&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M37-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Prepare a scientifically\ntraceable project submission with a report, presentation and\nself-critical assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Technical report<\/h2>\n<p>A scientific report is not a chronological record of every hour of\nwork. It guides the reader from the question through the method to the\nevidence and conclusions. University projects may require a report and\nan oral presentation (Curriculum G8/G9). The free Learning Courses\nproject adopts these working practices, but awards no university degree,\nuniversity grade or academic credits.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part<\/th>\n<th>Content and guiding question<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Title and abstract<\/td>\n<td>What was investigated and what was found?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Introduction and state of research<\/td>\n<td>Why is the question relevant? Which studies provide the\nfoundation?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Methods<\/td>\n<td>Can someone reconstruct the procedure using the data, equations and\nparameters?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Results<\/td>\n<td>Which metrics and checks were obtained?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discussion<\/td>\n<td>What alternative explanations and limitations exist?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conclusion<\/td>\n<td>What answer can be derived from the findings?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sources and appendix<\/td>\n<td>Can publications, data, scripts and versions be located?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Diagrams need axes with units, legible legends, correct scaling and a\nreference in the main text. Tables should present directly comparable\nquantities. Results and interpretation can be written separately; in the\nresults section, for example, we describe an RMSE of 1.041 K for the\nchanged model, whereas in the discussion we explain it as a consequence\nof the deliberately changed time constant.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_112_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Connections between the research question, computational archive, results, report and defence.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The evidence chain connects the research question,\nversioned computational data and findings with reporting and defence;\neach step should be traceable to its predecessor.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sample abstract for the completed teaching experiment<\/h2>\n<p>This study investigated whether a first-order dynamic thermal model\npredicts the temperature rise after a changed power step better than a\nstatic baseline. Both models were fitted to 31 synthetic observations at\n3 W and tested in a separate 5 W experiment. The dynamic model achieved\nan RMSE of 0.167 K compared with 2.634 K for the baseline and met both\npredefined thresholds. When the time constant was changed, the error\nrose to 1.041 K, exceeding the absolute acceptance limit. An analytical\nand a recursive calculation agreed numerically; 200 synthetic\nrepetitions quantified the variability of parameter estimation in the\nnominal model. The study demonstrates the suitability of the method\nunder the assumed conditions, not its validity for a real thermal\nplant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Short presentation and defence<\/h2>\n<p>As a practice format, ten minutes plus questions is a reasonable\nchoice, independently of a university’s formal requirements. Use, for\nexample, six slides: (1) problem and question, (2) model and\nassumptions, (3) experiment and data split, (4) comparison of results,\n(5) check under changed conditions and uncertainty, (6) conclusion and\nnext steps. Each slide should communicate one verifiable statement; show\nequations only when needed for interpretation. Explain the numerical\nvalues in their units and specify the measurement or simulation\nconditions. Have the data and computational commands ready for\nquestions.<\/p>\n<p>Possible examination questions: Why is the baseline fair even though\nit has no dynamics? Under which inputs is the model poorly identifiable?\nHow do the nominal test data differ from new real measurement data?\nWould a second-order model be useful, and how would you test it? A good\nanswer states both the evidence and its limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment and submission<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-30\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-40\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-30\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Points (max.)<\/th>\n<th>Evidence<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Question, hypothesis and literature<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td>Clearly defined subject and source basis<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Method and experimental plan<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td>Comparison, boundary conditions, metric<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reproducibility<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td>Code, data, settings, execution log<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validation and discussion<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">25<\/td>\n<td>Independent check, uncertainty, limitations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Report and short presentation<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">15<\/td>\n<td>Readability, reasoning, technical questions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The rubric is a suggested self-assessment, not a university\nexamination. Award points for evidenced work, not for a desired positive\nresult. A failed experiment can receive a very good assessment if the\nprocedure, explanation and limitations are traceable. Do not invent\nmeasurements or conceal incomplete tests. Document third-party code,\nlicences and AI assistance in accordance with the rules of your\ninstitution or publication venue.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Create your own submission with a research question, literature\nmatrix, experimental plan, executable model or dataset, reference\ncomparison, report and short presentation. <strong>Check:<\/strong> Use\nthe rubric above and refer to specific evidence.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give another person only the computational archive and instructions.\n<strong>Check:<\/strong> Can they generate the key numbers without manual\ntranscription? If no independent reviewer is available, document your\nown fresh run and mark external review as pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Formulate a conclusion with a limit of validity.\n<strong>Example:<\/strong> The dynamic model meets the error threshold\nfor the nominal synthetic test, but not with a changed time constant;\nvalidation against real measurements remains pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This concludes the study pathway in its English lesson version. Your\nown project work is a new, independent learning step and is not\nautomatically completed by reading these lessons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ee.ethz.ch/studies/master-s-programmes/main-master/projects-and-master-thesis.html\">ETH\nZürich – Projects and Master’s Thesis (Curriculum G8)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Semester\nprojects section: written report and oral presentation; university\nexample. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/compendia/\">The\nTuring Way – Research Compendia<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Research Compendia, Summary and\nMotivation: combining data, code and research documentation. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/reproducible-research/\">The\nTuring Way – Guide for Reproducible Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Guide for\nReproducible Research, introduction: reproducible analysis using\navailable data and code; methodological aspect. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
